To create a Laravel Jetstream Fortify app with Nginx, follow these steps:

1. Install Nginx: If you haven't already installed Nginx, you can install it using your package manager or by downloading the binary from the official website.
2. Create a new Laravel project: Start by creating a new Laravel project using the `composer` command. Make sure to specify the `--prefer-dist` flag to create a project with the latest dependencies.
```css
composer create-project --prefer-dist laravel/laravel <project-name>
```
3. Install Laravel Jetstream and Fortify: Next, you'll need to install the Laravel Jetstream and Fortify packages.
```javascript
composer require laravel/jetstream
composer require laravel/fortify
```
4. Configure Nginx: Next, you'll need to configure Nginx to serve your Laravel project. You can do this by creating a new configuration file in the `nginx` directory.

Here's an example configuration file:
```perl
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;

    server_name example.com;

    location / {
        root /var/www/html;
        index index.php;

        rewrite ^(.*)$ http://localhost:8000/$1 permanent;
    }

    location /api {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }
}
```
In this configuration file, you're telling Nginx to listen on port 80 and to redirect all requests to port 8000 on your Laravel project. You're also setting up a proxy\_pass for API routes to go to port 8000.
5. Start Nginx: Finally, you can start Nginx by running the following command:
```sql
sudo systemctl start nginx
```

That's it! Your Laravel project should now be accessible at <http://example.com>, and API routes should be proxied to port 8000.

