Here's an example of a function that deletes a node from a binary search tree (BST) in C:
```c
void deleteBST(struct Node* node) {
    if (node == NULL) {
        return;
    }
    if (node->left == NULL) {
        free(node);
    } else {
        deleteBST(node->left);
        deleteBST(node->right);
        free(node);
    }
}
```
This function takes a pointer to a `Node` struct as its input and first checks if the node is `NULL`. If it is, the function does nothing and returns.

If the node is not `NULL`, the function checks if the node has any children (i.e. if the left and right child pointers are both `NULL`). If the node has no children, the function frees the memory allocated for the node and returns.

If the node has children, the function recursively deletes the left and right children using the `deleteBST` function. After the children have been deleted, the function frees the memory allocated for the node and returns.

Note: This is a simple implementation of deleting a node from a BST and does not handle any edge cases or errors.

