Ceeling is a unit testing framework for the ESP32 and ESP8266 microcontrollers. Here's an example of how to use it to write and run unit tests for a simple function:

1. First, you need to install Ceeling on your computer. You can do this by following the instructions on the Ceeling GitHub page.
2. Next, you need to create a new project in Ceeling. You can do this by running the following command in the terminal:
```lua
ceedling init <project-name>
```
3. Then, you need to include the Ceeling header file in your source code:
```c
#include "Ceeling.h"
```
4. Now you can write your unit tests. A unit test is a function that calls the function you want to test and verifies that it returns the expected result. Here's an example of a unit test for a simple function `my_function()` that takes an integer argument and returns the square of that integer:
```scss
void my_function(int x) {
  return x * x;
}

TEST_CASE( "my_function tests", "[my_function]" ) {
  int result = my_function(4);
  TEST_ASSERT_EQUAL(result, 16);
}
```
5. After writing the test cases, you can run the test cases by running the following command in the terminal:
```
ceedling test
```
6. If all the test cases pass, then your function is working as expected. If any of the test cases fail, you will see an error message indicating which test case failed and what the expected and actual results were.

Note that this is just a simple example to give you an idea of how to use Ceeling for unit testing. In practice, you will likely want to write more complex tests that cover a wider range of scenarios and edge cases.

