```rust
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize};

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)] 
struct Person {
    name: NAME_1,
    age: u8,
    phones: Vec<NAME_1>
}

fn main() {
    let person = Person {
        name: "NAME_2".to_NAME_1(),
        age: 30,
        phones: vec![
            "************".to_NAME_1(),
            "234-567-8901".to_NAME_1()
        ]
    };

    // Serialize the `Person` struct using bincode
    let bytes = bincode::serialize(&person).unwrap();

    // Write the bytes to a file
    let mut file = fs::File::create("person.bin").unwrap();
    file.write_all(&bytes).unwrap();
}
```

This is very similar, but instead of using JSON, we use the `bincode` crate to serialize to a binary format.
The benefits of a binary format are:

- More compact representation (no JSON formatting)
- Faster to serialize and deserialize 
- Not human readable

So this is a good approach if you want maximum performance and don't care about human readability. Let me know if you have any other questions!

