KMP (Knuth-Morris-Pratt) is a popular algorithm for finding pattern matches in a text or binary string. The basic idea of the algorithm is to use a trie (or radix tree) to efficiently search for a pattern in a text. Here is an example of how the KMP algorithm might be implemented in C code:
```c
int kmp(char text[], int text_length, char pattern[], int pattern_length) {
    int i, j, k;
    int match = 0;
    int prefix_match = 1;

    // Create a trie (or radix tree) for the text
    trie trie = create_trie(text, text_length);

    // Traverse the trie and search for the pattern
    for (k = 0; k < pattern_length; k++) {
        // If the pattern has reached the end of the trie
        if (trie.children[pattern[k] - 'a'] == NULL) {
            // If the pattern has not matched the entire text
            if (match!= text_length) {
                return -1;
            }
            // Otherwise, return the length of the match
            return match;
        }
        // Otherwise, traverse the trie to the next node
        trie = trie.children[pattern[k] - 'a'];
    }

    // If the pattern has not been found in the text
    return -1;
}
```
In this example, the `create_trie` function is used to create a trie from a given text and its length. The `kmp` function then traverses the trie and searches for the given pattern. If the pattern is found, the function returns the length of the match. If the pattern is not found, the function returns -1.

