            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                // If the adjacent elements are in the wrong order, swap them
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
    // Print the sorted array
    for (int i : arr) {
        cout << i << " ";
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 2, 7, 1, 8, 4, 9};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubbleSort(arr, n);
    return 0;
}
```
In this implementation, we define a function `bubbleSort` that takes in an array `arr` and its size `n`. The function uses a nested loop to compare adjacent elements in the array and swap them if they are in the wrong order. The outer loop starts at the beginning of the array and ends at the second-to-last element, while the inner loop starts at the beginning of the array and ends at the current index minus one. This ensures that we compare and potentially swap adjacent elements.

After the sorting is done, we print out the sorted array using a range-based for loop.

