When an error occurs in your Express.js application and the error is caught by an error handler, you can pass the error data back to the controller using the `next()` function. The `next()` function allows you to pass the error data to the next middleware function in the chain or to the controller.

Here's an example of how you can pass the error data from an error handler back to the controller:
```javascript
app.use(function(err, req, res, next) {
  // Handle the error
  console.log(err);

  // Pass the error data back to the controller
  res.status(500).send({ error: err.message });
});

app.post('/login', function(req, res) {
  // Perform login logic
  if (authenticated) {
    // Redirect to the dashboard
    res.redirect('/dashboard');
  } else {
    // Return an error message
    res.status(401).send({ error: 'Invalid credentials' });
  }
});
```
In this example, the error handler is set up to handle any unhandled exceptions or errors that occur in the application. When an error occurs, the error handler logs the error to the console and sends a 500 Internal Server Error response to the client.

