Here is the bubble sort algorithm implemented in JavaScript:

```js
function bubbleSort(array) {
  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < array.length - i - 1; j++) {
      if (array[j] > array[j + 1]) {
        [array[j], array[j + 1]] = [array[j + 1], array[j]]
      }
    }
  }
  return array
}

let array = [5, 3, 2, 1, 4]
bubbleSort(array)
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

This works by iterating over the array multiple times. On each iteration, it compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. This "bubbles" the largest elements to the top.

Time complexity: O(n^2) since we have nested loops. 
Space complexity: O(1) since we sort the array in-place.

Here is bubble sort implemented in Rust:

```rust
fn bubble_sort(mut nums: Vec<i32>) -> Vec<i32> {
    let mut swapped = true;
    while swapped {
        swapped = false;
        for i in 0..nums.len() - 1 {
            if nums[i] > nums[i + 1] {
                nums.swap(i, i + 1);
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
    }
    nums
}

fn main() {
    let mut nums = vec![5, 3, 2, 1, 4];
    nums = bubble_sort(nums);
    println!("{:?}", nums); 
}
```

This works the same as the JavaScript version. We iterate over the array multiple times, comparing and swapping adjacent elements.

Some notes:

- We use a mutable vector `mut nums` since we sort in-place. 
- We have an outer loop that continues iterating until no swaps are made, indicated by the `swapped` boolean. 
- We use the `swap` method to swap elements in the vector.
- Rust's static typing and ownership rules ensure memory safety and efficiency.

Time complexity: O(n^2) 
Space complexity: O(1)

Here is an implementation of a red-black tree in JavaScript:

```js
class RedBlackTree {
  constructor() {
    this.root = null
  }

  insert(key) {
    let newNode = new Node(key)
    if (this.root == null) {
      this.root = newNode
      this.root.color = 'black'
      return
    }
    let current = this.root
    while (true) {
      if (key < current.key) {
        if (current.left == null) {
          current.left = newNode
          break
        }
        current = current.left
      } else {
        if (current.right == null) {
          current.right = newNode
          break
        } 
        current = current.right
      }
    }
    newNode.color = 'red'
    this.insertFix(newNode)
  }

  insertFix(node) {
    while (node != this.root && node.parent.color == 'red') {
      let parent = node.parent
      let grandParent = parent.parent

      if (parent == grandParent.left) {
        let uncle = grandParent.right
        if (uncle != null && uncle.color == 'red') {
          grandParent.color = 'red'
          parent.color = uncle.color = 'black'
          node = grandParent
        } else {
          if (node == parent.right) {
            this.leftRotate(parent)
            node = parent
            parent = node.parent
          } 
          grandParent.color = 'red'
          parent.color = 'black'
          this.rightRotate(grandParent)
        }
      } else {
        // Mirror of above with right and left swapped
        ...
      }
    }
    this.root.color = 'black'
  }
}
```

This implements the basic insert and insert fixup logic for a red-black tree. The insert fixup ensures that the tree maintains the red-black properties after an insertion.

Time complexity: O(log N) since it is a balanced binary search tree.
Space complexity: O(1)

