    // Send confirmation email
    // ...
  }

  public void sendFailedEmail(Payment payment) {
    // Send failed email
    // ...
  }
}
```
In this code, the `PaymentProcessor` class has three methods: `processPayment`, `sendConfirmationEmail`, and `sendFailedEmail`, which are all responsible for different things: processing payments, sending confirmation emails, and sending failed emails. This violates the Single Responsibility Principle, as the `PaymentProcessor` class has more than one reason to change.

Fix:
```java
class PaymentProcessor {
  private PaymentStatus status;

  public void processPayment(Payment payment) {
    // Validate payment details
    if (payment.amount > 0) {
      // Process payment
      this.status = PaymentStatus.PROCESSED;
    } else {
      // Invalid payment
      this.status = PaymentStatus.INVALID;
    }
  }

  public void sendConfirmationEmail(Payment payment) {
    // Send confirmation email
    // ...
  }

  public void sendFailedEmail(Payment payment) {
    // Send failed email
    // ...
  }
}
```
In this refactored code, the `PaymentProcessor` class has a single responsibility, which is to process payments. The `processPayment` method now updates the `status` property of the `PaymentProcessor` instance, which is the only reason for the class to change. This violation has been fixed by making the `PaymentProcessor` class more focused and less coupled to other classes, which makes it more robust and easier to maintain.

