2. Set up an interval to update the last activity time every few seconds. For example, you can use `setInterval(() => { lastActivity = Date.now(); }, 5000)`.
3. Create a function to check whether the user is inactive. You can do this by comparing the current time to the last activity time. For example, `if (Date.now() - lastActivity > 30000)` will check whether 30 seconds have passed since the last activity.
4. Add event listeners to reset the inactivity timer whenever the user interacts with the page. For example, you can add a click event listener to the body element with `document.body.addEventListener('click', resetInactivityTimer)`, or a keyup event listener to the document element with `document.documentElement.addEventListener('keyup', resetInactivityTimer)`.
5. Define the `resetInactivityTimer` function to reset the inactivity timer whenever an event is triggered. For example, `function resetInactivityTimer() { lastActivity = Date.now(); }` will update the last activity time to the current time.

Here's an example implementation of the `resetInactivityTimer` function:
```javascript
