The best way to handle exceptions in modern C# is using try/catch blocks with specific exception types. For example:

```csharp
try 
{
    // Some operation that may throw an exception
}
catch (IOException ex) 
{
    // Handle IOException specifically
}
catch (Exception ex) 
{
    // Handle general exceptions
}
```

You can also have multiple catch blocks to handle different exception types:

```csharp
try 
{
    // Some operation that may throw an exception
}
catch (FileNotFoundException ex) 
{
    // Handle file not found exception 
}
catch (IOException ex) 
{
    // Handle other IO exception
}
catch (Exception ex) 
{
    // Handle general exceptions
}
```

It's a good pattern to catch specific exceptions first, and have a general Exception catch block at the end to handle any other unforeseen exceptions.

You can also re-throw exceptions if needed using the throw keyword:

```csharp
try 
{
    // Some operation that may throw an exception
}
catch (FileNotFoundException ex) 
{
    logger.LogError(ex, "File not found");
    throw;  // Rethrow the exception
}
```

Some other options for exception handling in C# include:

- Using the Exception Filters to execute catch blocks based on exception type
- Defining custom exception types that inherit from Exception
- Using the finally block to execute code regardless of whether an exception occurred

So in summary, the best way is using specific catch blocks for known exception types, a general catch block for other exceptions, and re-throwing exceptions when needed. Let me know if you have any other questions!

Yes, in that case you can use a finally block. The code in the finally block will execute regardless of whether an exception occurred or not.

For example:
```csharp
try 
{
    // Some operation that may throw an exception
}
catch (FileNotFoundException ex) 
{
    // Handle file not found exception 
}
catch (IOException ex) 
{
    // Handle other IO exception
}
catch (Exception ex) 
{
    // Handle general exceptions
}
finally 
{
    // This will always execute
    Console.WriteLine("The finally block is executed");
}
```

No matter which catch block is executed (or if no exception occurs at all), the finally block will run. This is useful for cleanup code that you want to execute in all cases.

You can also have a finally block without any catch blocks:

```csharp
try 
{
    // Some operation that may throw an exception
}
finally 
{
    // This will always execute
    Console.WriteLine("The finally block is executed");
}
```

The finally block is a great way to avoid duplication in your exception handling code. You can put any common logic in the finally block, and it will run regardless of how the try/catch is executed.

Some things you may put in a finally block are:

- Closing database connections
- Releasing file locks
- Deleting temporary files
- Restoring state 
- Logging exceptions

So in summary, use a finally block to contain code that should always execute, regardless of how the try/catch blocks are executed. This avoids duplication and ensures important cleanup logic is always run.

