The correct way to assign the contents of one array to another in C++ is:

A loop to assign the elements of one array to the other array

Using just the array names with the assignment operator (=) or equality operator (==) will not copy the array elements.

For example:
```cpp
int arr1[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int arr2[5];

// Wrong - will not copy elements
arr2 = arr1;  

// Wrong - checks if arrays are equal, does not copy 
arr2 == arr1;  

// Correct - uses a loop to copy elements
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    arr2[i] = arr1[i];
}
```

Now arr2 will be {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}.

In C++, when you define an array, you are actually defining a pointer to the first element. So using = or == with two array names will simply compare or copy those pointers, not the actual array elements.

To copy the contents of one array to another, you need to iterate over the elements and copy each one individually. This can be done with a basic for loop, as shown in the example above.

So in summary:

- Just using array1 = array2 or array1 == array2 will not copy the array elements.
- You need to use a loop to copy each element individually.
- A for loop is a simple way to copy all elements of one array to another array.

