`` useEffect `` is a built-in hook in React that allows you to run code when a component's state or props change. It is essentially a wrapper around the `` useState `` hook that automatically cleans up after itself when the component unmounts.

Here's an example of how `` useEffect `` can be used to add a background image to a component that automatically disappears when the component unmounts:


```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const MyComponent = ({ image }) => {
  const [bgImage, setBgImage] = useState(new Image());

  useEffect(() => {
    bgImage.src = image;
  }, [image]);

  return (
    <div>
      <img src={image} />
      {bgImage && <img style={{ width: 'cover' }} />}
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;

```

In this example, we use the `` useEffect `` hook to set the `` src `` property of the `` bgImage `` state variable to `` image ``. When the component unmounts, the `` useEffect `` hook automatically cleans up and removes the `` bgImage `` element from the DOM, preventing it from being reused in other components.

Note that the `` useEffect `` hook is called after the component is rendered, so it is best used to add dynamic content that needs to be created after the component is mounted.

