The throw and catch keywords in C++ are used for exception handling. This allows you to "throw" an exception when an error occurs, and "catch" it at some later point to handle it.

For example:
```cpp
#include <stdexcept>

void divide(int a, int b) {
    if (b == 0) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Cannot divide by 0!");
    }
    int result = a / b;
    // ...
}

int main() {
    try {
        divide(10, 0);
    } catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {
        std::cout << e.what() << '\n';
    }
}
```

Here, the divide function will throw an invalid_argument exception if an attempt is made to divide by 0. The main function uses a try/catch block to catch this exception. The catch block will print the exception message, and the program will continue executing after the catch block.

If we did not have the try/catch, the program would crash when the exception is thrown. The catch block allows us to handle the exception gracefully and recover.

The general syntax is:

```
try {
    // code that may cause an exception
} catch (exception_type& e) {
    // handle exception
}
```

You can catch multiple exception types, and also catch generic exceptions with `catch (...)`. You can also re-throw caught exceptions with `throw;`.

Hope this helps explain the basics of exceptions in C++! Let me know if you have any other questions.

