Yes, Celery can be used with a worker built into a single initial process. This is known as a "single worker" setup.

In a single worker setup, the worker and the application are combined into a single process. This means that the worker will run in the same process as the application and will share the same memory space.

Here's an example of how to set up a single worker Celery app:
```python
from celery import Celery

app = Celery('tasks', broker='redis://localhost:6379/0')

@app.task
def worker_task():
    # Do some work here
    pass

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.conf.beat_schedule = {
        'send-tasks': {
            'worker_task',
        }
    }
    app.conf.task_queues = {
        'send-tasks': 'send-tasks',
    }
    app.worker_process_manager.start()
```
In this example, the `worker_task` is the task that will be executed by the worker, and it's scheduled to run using the `beat_schedule` configuration. The `task_queues` configuration is used to specify the queue name for the task.

When you run this code, the worker will be started and will run in the same process as the application. The worker will share the same memory space as the application and will be able to access the same data as the application.

One advantage of using a single worker setup is that it's simple to set up and doesn't require any additional configuration. However, it does have some limitations, such as the fact that the worker and the application can't run in separate processes, and the fact that the worker can't be scaled up to multiple processes.

