Unity provides some basic spawning logic in the  `GameObject.Find` function. This can be used to spawn game objects at a given location, in this case the **Scene** layer. This is commonly referred to as Spawners. A common use case for Spawners is to create pools of objects and cycle through them when needed.

Below is an example of using `GameObject.Find` to create a pool of `Player` game objects, and when needed return a random `Player` game object from the pool.

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class ExampleSpawner : MonoBehaviour {

  private static List<GameObject> playerPool = new List<GameObject>();

  // Use this for initialization
  void Start() {

  }

  // Update is called once per frame
  void Update() {

    // Spawn a random player
    GameObject player = (GameObject)GameObject.Find("Player");
    player.transform.position = new Vector3(Random.Range(-10, 10), 0, Random.Range(-10, 10));
    player.name = "Player" + Random.Range(0, 10);

    // If the list is empty, create a new player
    if (playerPool.Count == 0) {
      playerPool.Add(player.gameObject);
    } else {
      // Pick a player from the pool
      player = playerPool[Random.Range(0, playerPool.Count - 1)];
    }

    // Return the player to the pool
    playerPool.Remove(player);
    playerPool.Add(player.gameObject);

  }
}
```

There are some limitations to using the `GameObject.Find` Spawner, for example it only works on the **Scene** layer and only one instance of the game object can be returned.

