```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

char *reverse_string(char *str) {
    int length = strlen(str);
    char *new_str = malloc(length + 1);  // Allocate memory for the new string
    int i, j;

    // Reverse the characters of the string
    for (i = 0, j = length - 1; i < j; i++, j--) {
        char temp = new_str[i];
        new_str[i] = new_str[j];
        new_str[j] = temp;
    }

    // Add a null terminator to the end of the new string
    new_str[j] = '\0';

    // Return the new string
    return new_str;
}

int main() {
    char str[] = "hello";
    char *new_str = reverse_string(str);
    printf("Original string: %s\n", str);
    printf("Reversed string: %s\n", new_str);
    free(new_str);  // Free the memory allocated for the new string
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we use the `strlen` function to determine the length of the input string, and then allocate memory for the new string using `malloc`. We then use a `for` loop to reverse the characters of the string by swapping each pair of adjacent characters. Finally, we add a null terminator to the end of the new string and return it. In the `main` function, we test the function by passing a string and printing the original and reversed strings. Finally, we free the memory allocated for the new string using `free`.

