```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid;
    int status;
    char process_name[100];

    while ((pid = fork()) != -1) {
        if (pid == 0) {
            // Child process
            execl("/bin/cat", "cat", (char *)NULL);
        } else {
            // Parent process
            do {
                waitpid(pid, &status, WUNTRACED);
                printf("%d %s\n", pid, process_name);
            } while (WIFEXITED(status) == 0 && WEXITSTATUS(status) == 0);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This program forks off a new process repeatedly, using the `fork()` system call. In each child process, it runs the `cat` command to display the contents of the current process's memory, which will typically include a list of active processes. In each parent process, it waits for the child processes to terminate using the `waitpid()` system call, and then prints the process ID and name to the console.

Note that this program assumes that the `cat` command is available on the system, and that the user has permission to run it. Additionally, the program may not list all active processes, as some processes may be running with lower privileges or may be hidden from the user.

