Even if I not using created object, server returns 500 Internal Server Error and tell me:
Whoops!
We seem to have hit a snag. Please try again later...
What Im doing wrong?

This is happening because in CodeIgniter 4, controllers are instantiated as singletons by default. This means that only one instance of the controller is created, and it is reused for all requests.

Because of this, when you try to instantiate the `StubWelcomeMessageProvider` class within the controller method, it actually persists between requests and causes issues.

There are a couple ways to fix this:

1. Make the controller non-singleton. You can do this by adding `public $singleton = false;` at the top of the controller:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Controllers;

class Home extends BaseController 
{
    public $singleton = false;

    public function index()
    {
        $welcomeMessageProvider = new StubWelcomeMessageProvider();
        return 'hello world';
    }
}
```

This will instantiate a new controller instance for every request, fixing the issue.

2. Instantiate the class outside of the controller method. For example:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Controllers;

use Services\StubWelcomeMessageProvider;

class Home extends BaseController 
{
    protected $provider;

    public function __construct()
    {
        $this->provider = new StubWelcomeMessageProvider();
    }

    public function index()
    {
        return 'hello world';
    }
}
```

Here we instantiate the `StubWelcomeMessageProvider` in the constructor, outside of any request method. This is safe since the constructor is only called once when the singleton controller is initialized.

Then we can access `$this->provider` in our methods.

