Podman is a NAME_1-compatible container engine that can be used as an alternative to NAME_1. If you're running Podman on a machine without internet access, you can follow similar steps as described above for NAME_1 to load images from a local registry or cache.

1. Local Podman Registry:
If you have a local Podman registry set up on your machine, you can pull images from there. To do this, you'll need to configure your Podman daemon to use the local registry. You can do this by adding the following options to your Podman daemon's configuration file (usually located at `~/.podman/podman.conf`):
```json
{
  "runtimes": {
    "io.podman.NAME_1": {
      "options": {
        "registry.url": "http://localhost:5000"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
Replace `localhost:5000` with the appropriate values for your local registry. You can then pull images from the local registry as you would from any other registry.

1. Podman Cache:
Another option is to cache frequently used images on your machine. To do this, you can create a directory where Podman will store cached images. By default, this directory is located at `/var/lib/podman/cache`. You can configure the cache size and other options by setting the `PODMAN_CACHE_SIZE` environment variable.

To cache an image, you can use the `--cache-opt` or `-c` option when running `podman pull` or `podman run`. For example, to cache the `ubuntu` image, you can run:
```r
podman pull --cache-opt=dir=my-cache-dir ubuntu
```
This will store a copy of the `ubuntu` image in the `my-cache-dir` directory.

Keep in mind that caching images can consume significant disk space, so you'll need to monitor the cache size and delete cached images as needed.

