In C language, floating-point numbers and integers are two different data types used to store numerical values. The main differences between these two data types are:

1. Range: Floating-point numbers have a much larger range than integers. Floating-point numbers can represent decimal values with a precision of up to 10 decimal places, whereas integers can only represent whole numbers.
2. Representation: Floating-point numbers are represented in memory using a floating-point format that occupies twice the amount of memory as an integer. This is because floating-point numbers require a sign bit, an exponent field, and a mantissa field to represent their value. In contrast, integers are stored as a single value in memory.
3. Arithmetic operations: Floating-point numbers support arithmetic operations with decimal values, such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. In contrast, integers only support arithmetic operations with whole numbers.

