Here's an example of a sticky section animation with GSAP:

```js
// Select elements
const section = document.querySelector("#section");
const header = document.querySelector("#header");

// GSAP timeline
const tl = new TimelineMax();

// Initial position of section 
tl.set(section, { y: "100%" });

// Animation
tl.to(section, { 
  y: "0%", 
  ease: "power2.out", 
  duration: 1 
});

// Make header sticky 
tl.to(header, {
  position: "fixed",
  top: 0,
  ease: "power2.out"
});

// ScrollTrigger
let st = new ScrollTrigger.Scene({
  triggerElement: section,
  duration: "200%"
})
.setTween(tl)
.reverse(false)   // Repeat on scroll up
.addTo(ScrollTrigger);
```

This does the following:

- Selects the #section element and #header element
- Creates a GSAP TimelineMax instance
- Initially positions the #section off screen
- Animates #section into view 
- Makes the #header element sticky as #section scrolls into view
- Uses ScrollTrigger to trigger the animation on scroll
- Repeats the animation on scroll up

