That error is occurring because you're trying to use a literal operator (_xor) that hasn't been defined.

A literal operator allows you to define a custom suffix for literals. For example, the std::string literal operator allows you to write:

```cpp
std::string s = "Hello"_s;
```

Instead of:

```cpp
std::string s = std::string("Hello");
```

To define your own literal operator, you use the operator"" syntax. For example, to define a _xor literal operator, you'd do:

```cpp
std::string operator""_xor(const char* str, std::size_t len) {
    // ...
    return encoded_string;
}
```

Then you could use it like:

```cpp 
std::string dadasda = "https://remotecarefreepasswords.graveslor.repl.co/"_xor;
```

So to fix that error, you need to define the _xor literal operator. A basic implementation could be:

```cpp
std::string operator""_xor(const char* str, std::size_t len) {
    std::string result;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
        result += char(str[i] ^ 0x12);
    }
    return result;
}
```

This will XOR each character in the input string with 0x12, and return the encoded string.

