Sure! Here's an example of how you can re-write component children using `createPortal` in React:
```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from'react';

function MyComponent() {
  const portalRef = React.createRef();

  useEffect(() => {
    const div = document.createElement('div');
    document.body.appendChild(div);
    portalRef.current = ReactDOM.createPortal(div, document.body);
    div.innerHTML = 'Hello from Portal!';
  }, []);

  return (
    <>
      <div ref={portalRef}>
        <h1>Hello World!</h1>
        <p>This is some text.</p>
      </div>
    </>
  );
}
```
In this example, we create a `portalRef` using `React.createRef()`. We then use `ReactDOM.createPortal` to render the children of `MyComponent` into a `div` element that we add to the document body. We store a reference to this `div` element in the `portalRef` using `ReactDOM.createPortal`.

Inside the `useEffect` hook, we create another `div` element and set its `innerHTML` to `'Hello from Portal!'`. We then append this `div` element to the `portalRef` element created using `ReactDOM.createPortal`.

Finally, we render the children of `MyComponent` as before, but this time they are rendered within the `div` element created using `createPortal`.

