To run another process from C and send data to it through standard input (stdin), you can use the `fork()` system call to create a new process and `execlp()` to execute a command in that process. Here's an example:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    int pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        // This is the child process
        execlp("cat", "cat", (char *)NULL);
    } else {
        // This is the parent process
        char *args[] = {"ls", (char *)NULL};
        execvp(args[0], args);
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, `fork()` creates a new process that is a copy of the current process. The child process continues executing the code after the `fork()` call, while the parent process returns to the calling process.

In the child process, `execlp("cat", "cat", (char *)NULL)` replaces the current process with the `cat` command, which prints its arguments to standard output.

In the parent process, `execvp(args[0], args)` replaces the current process with the `ls` command, which prints a list of files in the current directory.

Both the child and parent processes are executed concurrently, and the parent process can send data to the child process through standard input. In this example, the child process reads its standard input from the terminal, so you can type commands and see their output in real time.

Here's an example of a C program that sends data to a child process through standard input:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main() {
    int pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        // This is the child process
        char buf[1024];
        memset(buf, 0, sizeof(buf));
        while (1) {
            if (read(0, buf, sizeof(buf)) > 0) {
                printf("Received data: %s\n", buf);
            }
        }
    } else {
        // This is the parent process
        char *args[] = {"sh", "-c", "echo hello world"};
        execvp(args[0], args);
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the parent process creates a new child process using `fork()`, and the child process reads data from the standard input stream using `read()`. The parent process sends the string "hello world" to the child process through standard input.

In the parent process, `execvp(args[0], args)` replaces the current process with a new process that runs the shell command `echo hello world`.

When you run this program, you can see the output of the `echo` command in the terminal, as well as the message "Received data: hello world" printed by the child process.

Note that the standard input of the child process is connected to the standard output of the parent process, so any data sent to the standard output by the parent process will be received by the child process as input.

