Those are great questions! Here are my thoughts:

1. In terms of correctness and performance, it depends on your use case. Some guidelines:

- Use Transient for lightweight, stateless services. This gives the best performance since a new instance is created every request.
- Use NAME_1 for services that should be reused within a request, but need a separate instance per request. This is a good default. 
- Use Singleton for services that can be reused for the lifetime of the app. Only use this for truly stateless, thread-safe services.

So I would say in general, NAME_1 is a good default and provides a good balance of correctness and performance for most use cases. Only use Transient or Singleton when you have a specific need.

2. A scope created with IServiceScopeFactory.CreateScope() will NOT be automatically disposed. You have to dispose it manually. If you don't dispose it, the services within that scope will live for the lifetime of the request.

So in your example, if you did:
```csharp
var scope = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService<IServiceScopeFactory>()
                .CreateScope(); 
var userRepository = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IUserRepository>();
// Use userRepository... 
// Forget to dispose scope!
```

Then userRepository would live until the end of the request, even though you're no longer using it. This can cause memory issues if you create a lot of scopes and don't dispose them.

So you should always dispose scopes when you're done with them, using a `using` statement or by calling `Dispose()` directly:

```csharp 
using (var scope = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService<IServiceScopeFactory>()
                .CreateScope())
{
    var userRepository = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IUserRepository>();
    // Use userRepository
