Sure! Here's an example of a class that implements IDisposable properly:
```csharp
public class MyScopedService : IDisposable
{
    private bool _disposed;

    public MyScopedService()
    {
    }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        // Use other NAME_1 services...
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(true);
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);
    }

    protected virtual void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (!_disposed)
        {
            if (disposing)
            {
                // Dispose managed resources
            }

            // Dispose unmanaged resources
        }

        _disposed = true;
    }

    ~MyScopedService()
    {
        Dispose(false);
    }
}
```

Some things to note:

- The class implements IDisposable and has a Dispose() method.
- It has a boolean `_disposed` to track if it's been disposed.
- The Dispose() method calls Dispose(true) to dispose managed and unmanaged resources, and then suppresses the finalizer.
- The Dispose(bool) method checks `_disposed` to make sure resources aren't disposed twice.
- The finalizer calls Dispose(false) to clean up unmanaged resources in case the user forgot to call Dispose(). 
- You would call `Dispose()` on this class when the scope ends, to properly clean it up.

