In EF Core, you can model a complex entity without a foreign key relation using:

1. An owned entity type
2. A navigation property

For example, say you have a Customer entity and a complex Address entity. You can model it like this:

```csharp
public class Customer
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public Address Address { get; set; }
}

public class Address
{
    public string Street { get; set; }
    public string City { get; set; }
    public string State { get; set; }
    public string ZipCode { get; set; }
} 
```

Then in your DbContext, configure Address as an owned entity:

```csharp
public class MyDbContext : DbContext
{
    public DbSet<Customer> Customers { get; set; }

    protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>()
            .OwnsOne(c => c.Address);
    }
}
```

This will:

- Store the Address fields in the same table as Customer
- Use a naming convention to prefix the Address fields (e.g. Address_Street)
- Automatically update/delete the Address fields when Customer is updated/deleted

So you have a complex owned entity mapped without a foreign key. You can access it through simple navigation:

```csharp
var customer = context.Customers.Find(1);
var address = customer.Address;
```

