CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a security feature implemented by web browsers that blocks web pages from making requests to a different domain than the one that served the web page. This is a security feature to prevent malicious scripts from accessing sensitive information and performing unauthorized actions on behalf of the user.

CORS exists to prevent malicious websites from making unauthorized requests to your website on behalf of the user. For example, if a malicious website can make a request to your website, it can potentially access sensitive information or perform actions on behalf of the user without their consent.

To handle CORS in Node.js, you can use the built-in `cors` middleware package. Here is an example of how to use it:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');

const app = express();

app.use(cors());

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.send('Hello World!');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port 3000');
});
```
In this example, the `app.use(cors())` line adds the `cors` middleware to the application. This middleware automatically adds the necessary response headers to allow cross-origin requests.

You can also specify additional options for the `cors` middleware by passing in an options object as the second argument. For example, you can specify the allowed origins, methods, and headers by passing in an object like this:
```javascript
app.use(cors({
  origin: 'http://example.com',
  methods: 'GET,POST,PUT,DELETE',
  headers: 'Content-Type'
}));
```
In this example, only requests from `http://example.com` will be allowed, and only the `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, and `DELETE` methods will be allowed. The `Content-Type` header will also be allowed.

Here's a basic macOS app that can convert human input to JSON:

```swift
import Cocoa

class JSONConverter: NSWindowController {

    @IBOutlet weak var inputTextView: NSTextView!
    @IBOutlet weak var outputTextView: NSTextView!

    override func windowDidLoad() {
        super.windowDidLoad()
    }

    @IBAction func convertToJSON(_ sender: Any) {
        let input = inputTextView.string
        let data = input.data(using: .utf8)
        do {
            let json = try JSONSerialization.jsonObject(with: data!, options: [])
            let prettyJsonData = try JSONSerialization.data(withJSONObject: json, options: .prettyPrinted)
            let prettyJson = String(data: prettyJsonData, encoding: .utf8)
            outputTextView.string = prettyJson
        } catch let error {
            print(error)
        }
    }
}
```

