One approach to implementing this in Rust would be to use a trait that defines the common field types and their methods, and then use this trait to define a generic parser that can handle any field type.

Here's an example of how to define the trait and the parser:


```rust
use std::str;
use std::fs;
use std::path::Path;

trait Field {
    fn get_value(&self) -> &str;
}

trait FieldParser {
    fn from_str(field: &Field, value: &str) -> Field;
}

struct Point {
    x: i32,
    y: i32,
}

struct IdField(Field);
struct TimeField(Field);

fn id_field(field: Field) -> IdField {
    IdField {
        field: field,
    }
}

fn time_field(field: Field) -> TimeField {
    TimeField {
        field: field,
    }
}

struct Example(String, String, String, String);

fn example_field(field: Field) -> Example {
    Example(field.get_value(), field.get_value(), field.get_value(), field.get_value())
}

fn parse_file(file_path: &str) -> Result<Vec<Field>, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    fs::read_to_end(Path::new(file_path)).map_err(|e| Box::new(e))?;
    let mut fields: Vec<Field> = Vec::new();
    for line in fs::read_to_end(Path::new(file_path)).map_err(|e| Box::new(e))?; {
        let field = Field::new(Example(line.trim().to_string(), line.trim().to_string(), line.trim().to_string(), line.trim().to_string()));
        fields.push(field);
    }
    Ok(fields)
}

fn main() {
    let fields = parse_file("events.csv").unwrap();
    for field in fields {
        println!("{}", field);
    }
}

```

In this example, `` Field `` and `` FieldParser `` are traits that define the common field types and their methods. The `` id_field() `` and `` time_field() `` functions provide implementations of these traits for the `` IdField `` and `` TimeField `` types, respectively. The `` example_field() `` function provides an implementation of the `` Field `` trait for the `` Example `` type, which represents a field that holds a string value.

The `` parse_file() `` function reads the CSV file, reads each line, and creates a `` Field `` instance for each line. It then adds the `` Field `` instances to a `` Vec ``. The `` main() `` function calls `` parse_file() `` to get the list of fields, and then prints each field value to the console.

This implementation provides a generic way to handle any field type, and allows you to easily add new field types and update existing ones without having to change the main code.

