In C++, a `variant` is a type-safe wrapper around a value that can hold any one of a set of possible types. The `std::variant` class template is defined in the C++17 standard, and provides a way to create a variant that can hold a value of a specific set of types.

Here's an example of how to use a `std::variant` in C++17:
```c
#include <iostream>
#include <variant>

int main() {
  std::variant<int, double, std::string> v;
  v = 42;
  std::cout << std::get<int>(v) << std::endl;
  v = 3.14;
  std::cout << std::get<double>(v) << std::endl;
  v = "hello";
  std::cout << std::get<std::string>(v) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```
In this example, we have a `std::variant<int, double, std::string>` called `v`. We can assign a value of type `int` to `v`, a value of type `double` to `v`, and a value of type `std::string` to `v`. We can then use the `std::get` function to extract the value of the specific type stored in the variant.

The `std::variant` class template is useful when you need to store a value of a specific set of types, and you want to ensure that the value is of one of those types, without having to check the type at runtime. This can help avoid runtime errors caused by type conversions or pointing to the wrong part of memory.

To use `std::variant` with COM in C++17, you need to include the `<comdef.h>` header and use the `COMVARIANT` class template. Here's an example of how to use `COMVARIANT`:
```c
#include <iostream>
#include <comdef.h>
#include <variant>

HRESULT GetVariantValue(const COMVARIANT& variant, int* pInt, double* pDouble, std::string* pString)
{
  switch (variant.vt)
  {
    case VT_INT:
      *pInt = variant.intVal;
      return S_OK;
    case VT_DOUBLE:
      *pDouble = variant.doubleVal;
      return S_OK;
    case VT_STRING:
      *pString = variant.bstrVal;
      return S_OK;
    default:
      return E_FAIL;
  }
}

int main()
{
  COMVARIANT variant;
  variant.intVal = 42;
