```c
#include <stdint.h>

// Montgomery multiplication
void montgomery_mul(uint64_t a, uint64_t b, uint64_t p, uint64_t m, uint64_t *r) {
    uint64_t t = b;
    uint64_t c = a * t;
    uint64_t u = (a * b) % p;
    uint64_t v = (t * c - u * a) % p;
    uint64_t w = (v + m) % p;
    *r = (c + w) % p;
}

// Modular addition
void modular_add(uint64_t a, uint64_t b, uint64_t p, uint64_t *r) {
    uint64_t c = a + b;
    if (c >= p) {
        c -= p;
        *r = 1;
    }
}

// Modular subtraction
void modular_sub(uint64_t a, uint64_t b, uint64_t p, uint64_t *r) {
    uint64_t c = a - b;
    if (c < 0) {
        c += p;
        *r = -1;
    }
}
```
Note that this implementation uses the Montgomery ladder algorithm for modular multiplication, which is a relatively efficient algorithm for moduli of the form `p = 2^k - 1`. For larger moduli, other algorithms such as the celebrated-algorithm or the Miller-Rabin algorithm may be more efficient. Additionally, this implementation assumes that the inputs `a`, `b`, `p`, and `m` are all 32-bit or 64-bit integers, and may need to be modified for other types or sizes of integers.

