1. Convert your UIViewControllers to SwiftUI Views. For example:

```swift
class MyViewController: UIViewController {
    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        let label = UILabel()
        label.text = "Hello"
        view.addSubview(label)
    }
}
```

Would become:

```swift
struct MyView: View {
    var body: some View {
        Text("Hello")
    }
}
```

2. Remove your storyboard and add SwiftUI views to your app's content. For example in AppDelegate:

```swift
@UIApplicationMain
class AppDelegate: UIResponder, UIApplicationDelegate {
    var window: UIWindow?

    func application(_ application: UIApplication, didFinishLaunchingWithOptions launchOptions: [UIApplication.LaunchOptionsKey: Any]?) -> Bool {
        // Remove storyboard instantiation
        window = UIWindow(frame: UIScreen.main.bounds)

        // Add SwiftUI view
        window?.rootViewController = UIHostingController(rootView: MyView())

        window?.makeKeyAndVisible()
        return true
    }
} 
```

3. For navigation between views, use SwiftUI's NavigationView and NavigationLink instead of UIStoryboardSegues.

4. Use the MVVM pattern by creating ViewModels for your SwiftUI Views. For example:

```swift
struct MyViewModel {
    // Model
    let name = "NAME_1"
}

struct MyView: View {
    @ObservedObject var viewModel = MyViewModel()

    var body: some View {
        Text(viewModel.name)
    }
}
```

5. You can also use Coordinators to manage the navigation flow between views, similar to how a storyboard does.

That covers the basic steps to convert from UIKit storyboards to SwiftUI with MVVM. Let me know if you have any other questions!

