  }
});

export default MyApiController;

```

In this example, the `` MyApiController `` defines a `` get `` route that returns the string "Hello, world!". The `` get `` method is used to handle HTTP GET requests to the `` /api/hello `` endpoint.

You can also define `` post `` routes using the `` Controller `` class and the `` post `` method:


```javascript
const MyApiController = Controller({
  post: (req, res) => {
    const data = JSON.parse(req.body.data);
    res.send(`Hello, ${data.name}!`);
  }
});

export default MyApiController;

```

In this example, the `` MyApiController `` defines a `` post `` route that takes a JSON-encoded object containing a `` name `` property. The `` post `` method is used to handle HTTP POST requests to the `` /api/hello `` endpoint.

By using the `` Controller `` class, you can define API routes in a simple and straightforward way, without having to write code to handle the low-level details of HTTP requests and responses. This makes it easy to build and manage API-driven applications in Next.JS.

