```rust
use std::str::CharIndices;

fn tokenize_twitter_text(text: &str) -> Vec<&str> {
    let mut tokens: Vec<&str> = text.split('/').collect();

    let mut char_indices = CharIndices::from_chars(text);
    let mut token_start = 0;
    loop {
        let token_end = match char_indices.get(token_start..).unwrap() {
            "\n" => token_start + 1,
            c if c.is_whitespace() || c.is_ascii_punctuation() => token_start + 1,
            _ => token_start,
        };
        if token_end == token_start {
            break;
        }
        let token = &text[token_start..token_end];
        tokens.push(token);
        token_start = token_end;
    }

    tokens
}
```
This function takes a string representing a Twitter text as input and returns a vector of strings, each representing a token in the text.

The function first splits the input string on '/' characters to get the "tweet" and "username" parts of the text. It then iterates over each character in the text, keeping track of where each token starts and ends. When it encounters a newline character, it assumes that the next token will start on the next line. When it encounters a non-whitespace or non-punctuation character, it assumes that the current token ends. If the function encounters a character that is neither whitespace nor punctuation, it assumes that the token ends at that point.

The function continues this process until it has processed all characters in the text, creating a vector of tokens in the process.

Note that this function is not perfect and may not handle all cases correctly. For example, it does not handle hashtags, which are represented by the "#" character in Twitter texts. Additionally, it does not handle retweets or replies, which may not be represented in a standard Twitter text.

