```c
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <cstring>

using namespace std;

void scrambleString(char *str) {
    int length = strlen(str);
    for (int i = 0; i < length / 2; i++) {
        int j = rand() % (length - i);
        char temp = str[i];
        str[i] = str[j];
        str[j] = temp;
    }
}

int main() {
    char str[100];
    cout << "Enter a string: ";
    cin >> str;
    scrambleString(str);
    cout << "Scrambled string: " << str << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this program, the `scrambleString` function takes a pointer to a character array as input and scrambles the characters of the string randomly. The function uses the `rand()` function from the standard library to generate a random index, and then swaps the characters at that index with another random index. The `main` function simply prompts the user to enter a string, calls the `scrambleString` function to scramble the string, and then prints the scrambled string.

