Promises: Promise chains can become long and hard to read when more asynchronous operations are involved. This can lead to a phenomenon called "callback hell" or "promise chaining hell."

Async/Await: Async/Await provides a more synchronous-like code style that is easier to read and understand, even when multiple asynchronous operations are involved. It helps in writing cleaner and more maintainable code.

In summary, both Promises and Async/Await serve the same purpose of handling asynchronous operations in JavaScript, but Async/Await offers a more modern and cleaner syntax along with better readability and traditional error handling. However, it is important to note that Async/Await is built on top of Promises, and they can be used together when necessary.

