Here's a simple function in C that approximates the square root of its argument x using the Babylonian method, also known as Herons method or the method of successive averaging. This method involves iterative approximation, where the result is continually refined by averaging the previous estimate with the new estimate derived from it.

The function takes a floating-point number x as an argument and returns the approximate square root of x.
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

double approx_sqrt(double x) {
    double x_squared = x * x;
    double a = x;
    double b = 0;
    double c = x;
    int iter = 0;

    while (iter < 100) {
        double na = a + b;
        double nc = (na * na - x_squared) / (2 * na);

        a = na;
        b = nc;
        c = na + nc;
        x_squared = c * c;

        iter++;
    }

    return c;
}

int main() {
    double x = 100.0;
    double sqrt_x = approx_sqrt(x);
    printf("Approximate square root of %lf: %.6f\n", x, sqrt_x);
    return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses a simple loop with a fixed number of iterations (in this case, 100). You can adjust the number of iterations to improve the accuracy of the result, but keep in mind that more iterations will also increase the computation time.

