Yes, you can bind the JSON text messages from your Azure queue to an Azure Function as an object of a specific type rather than a string. You can achieve this by specifying the input parameter type as `IModel` and using the `AsModel` method to convert the JSON text into an object of the desired type. Here's an example of how you can do this in.NET Core 6 and Azure Functions v4:

1. Create the pipeline and the Azure Function:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Azure.WebJobs;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

public static class QueueTriggerFunction
{
    public static IJobHost CreateJobHost(IWebJobsBuilder webJobsBuilder)
    {
        return webJobsBuilder.Create<QueueTriggerFunction>();
    }

    [FunctionName("QueueTriggerFunction")]
    public static async Task<IActionResult> Run(
        [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Function, "post", Route = null)]
        [ApiController]
        IModel model,
        ILogger log)
    {
        // Get the JSON text message from the input
        var jsonText = model.Value as string;

        // Convert the JSON text into an object
        var obj = Convert.FromJson(jsonText, Newtonsoft.Json.Linq.JArray. sizeof(string));

        // Log the object as metadata
        log.LogInformation($"Received message: {obj.ToString()}");

        // Do something with the object...
        //...

        return Ok();
    }
}
```
1. Modify the `QueueTriggerFunction` to use the `AsModel` method:
```csharp
[FunctionName("QueueTriggerFunction")]
public static async Task<IActionResult> Run(
    [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Function, "post", Route = null)]
    [ApiController]
    IModel model,
    ILogger log)
    {
        // Get the JSON text message from the input
        var jsonText = model.Value as string;

        // Convert the JSON text into an object
        var obj = Convert.FromJson(jsonText, Newtonsoft.Json.Linq.JArray. sizeof(string));

        // Use the `AsModel` method to convert the object into a specific type
        var modelType = JObject.Parse(obj).ToObject<IModelType>(c => c.Name = "MyCustomType");
        var objModel = (IModel)modelType.Value;

        // Log the object as metadata
        log.LogInformation($"Received message: {objModel.ToString()}");

        // Do something with the object...
        //...

        return Ok();
    }
```
In the modified example, the input parameter `model` is of type `IModel` and is passed to the `QueueTriggerFunction` using the `AsModel` method. The `AsModel` method parses the JSON text from the input parameter and converts it into an object of the `IModelType` specified in the `IModel` property. This allows you to bind the input parameter to an Azure Function as an object of a specific type.

