In Go, you can use the `net/http` package to send an HTTP request to a server. Here's a simple example of how to send a GET request to a server and print the response:
```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"log"
	"net/http"
)

func main() {
	// Create an HTTP client
	client := &http.Client{}

	// Set up the request
	req, err := http.NewRequest("GET", "https://api.example.com/data", nil)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	// Add necessary headers
	req.Header.Add("User-Agent", "Go-HTTP-Client")

	// Send the request
	resp, err := client.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()

	// Read the response body
	body, err := ioutil.ReadAll(resp.Body)
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	// Print the response status and body
	fmt.Printf("Status code: %d\n", resp.StatusCode)
	fmt.Printf("Body: %s\n", body)
}
```
In this example, we create an HTTP client with `http.DefaultClient` (which uses the default system http.Client). We then create a `http.Request` object with the "GET" method, specifying the URL we want to request.

We add a User-Agent header to the request to identify our client. The `http.Client.Do()` method sends the request and returns a `http.Response` object. We can read the response body using `ioutil.ReadAll()`, and print the status code and body of the response.

Please replace "<https://api.example.com/data>" with the actual URL you want to make a request to. Note that making requests to websites other than your own may violate their terms of service, and you should always be cautious about what data you're accessing.

