Sure! Here's a quick sort function in Kotlin that sorts an array of integers in ascending order:
```kotlin
fun quickSort(arr: Array<Int>): Array<Int> {
    if (arr.size < 2) {
        // if the array has less than 2 elements, it's already sorted, so return it as is
        return arr
    }

    // choose the first element as the pivot
    val pivot = arr[0]

    // partition the array around the pivot
    val lessThanPivot = mutableListOf<Int>()
    val greaterThanPivot = mutableListOf<Int>()
    for (i in 1..arr.size - 1) {
        if (arr[i] < pivot) {
            lessThanPivot.add(arr[i])
        } else if (arr[i] > pivot) {
            greaterThanPivot.add(arr[i])
        }
    }

    // recursively sort the left and right halves of the array
    val lessThanHalf = quickSort(lessThanPivot)
    val greaterThanHalf = quickSort(greaterThanPivot)

    // concatenate the sorted left and right halves with the pivot
    return arrayOf(pivot).concat(lessThanHalf).concat(greaterThanHalf)
}
```
Here's how you can use this function to sort an array of integers:
```kotlin
val arr = intArrayOf(5, 2, 6, 1, 3, 4)
val sortedArr = quickSort(arr)
println(sortedArr) // prints [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
```
The quick sort algorithm works by choosing a pivot element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The sub-arrays are then recursively sorted, and the sorted sub-arrays are concatenated to produce the final sorted array.

In this implementation, the pivot is chosen as the first element of the array. If the array has less than 2 elements, it's already sorted, so the function returns the array as is.

