The strangler pattern is a design pattern in computer science that is used to replace a system's original classes with a new set of classes. The new classes are created by subclassing the original classes and modifying their behavior. This process is repeated until the original classes are completely replaced by the new classes.

The strangler pattern can be used in a variety of situations, including when a system's original classes are outdated or no longer support the desired behavior, or when a new system needs to be developed with a similar functionality.

Here is an example of the strangler pattern:


```php
public class OriginalClass {
    public void method() {
        // original behavior
    }
}

public class StranglerClass extends OriginalClass {
    public void method() {
        // new behavior
    }
}

public class StranglerClass2 extends StranglerClass {
    public void method() {
        // new behavior
    }
}

```

In this example, `` OriginalClass `` contains the original behavior of the system. `` StranglerClass `` and `` StranglerClass2 `` modify the behavior of `` OriginalClass `` to create new classes with the desired behavior. The process is repeated until the original classes are completely replaced by the new classes.

