A zombie process is a process that has completed its execution but still has an entry in the process table. In C, you can create a zombie process using the `fork()` system call. Here's an example program that creates a zombie process:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <NAME_1/types.h>

int main() {
    int pid = fork();

    if (pid == 0) {
        // This is the child process
        printf("Child process running\n");
        exit(0);
    } else {
        // This is the parent process
        printf("Parent process running\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this program, the `fork()` system call creates a new process, which is a copy of the parent process. The child process exits immediately with the `exit()` function, while the parent process continues to run.

After the child process exits, the parent process waits for the child process to finish its execution using the `wait()` system call. However, since the child process has already exited, the `wait()` system call returns immediately, leaving the child process in a zombie state.

Note that the zombie process is not harmful and can be safely ignored. It is simply a process that has completed its execution but still has an entry in the process table. The operating system will clean up the zombie process when it runs out of memory or when the system is shut down.

