In PHP, variables can be included in double-quoted or single-quoted strings using the dollar sign ($) to denote the start of the variable name. However, the behavior of variable expansion in strings depends on the type of quotes used.

If you use double quotes to enclose a string, any variables included in the string will be expanded to their value when the string is evaluated. For example:
```bash
$name = 'NAME_1';
$title = 'Mr.';

$greeting = "Hello, $name $title";
echo $greeting;
```
This will output "Hello, NAME_1 Mr.".

On the other hand, if you use single quotes to enclose a string, variables will not be expanded to their value. Any variables included in the string will be treated as literal values. For example:
```bash
$name = 'NAME_1';
$title = 'Mr.';

$greeting = 'Hello, $name $title';
echo $greeting;
```
This will output "Hello, $name $title".

It is important to note that the use of single quotes can result in unexpected behavior if you are trying to include variables in your string. To ensure that variables are expanded to their value, it is recommended to use double quotes.

