In PHP, all variables within the scope of a function will lose their values once the function has finished executing. This is known as the "function call stack" behavior, where variables are created and destroyed as a function calls other functions.

When a function is called, a new scope is created for the variables used within the function. These variables are only accessible within the function and are destroyed when the function returns. Any variables with the same name in outer scopes are not affected by the function call.

For example:
```php
function foo() {
    $x = 1;
    echo $x;
}

foo();
echo $x;
```
In this example, the `foo()` function assigns the value `1` to the variable `$x` within its scope. When the function returns, the variable `$x` is destroyed, and any further references to `$x` will result in an error. When the `foo()` function is called again, a new instance of `$x` is created with a value of `1`, and the second `echo` statement will output `1`.

It is important to note that this behavior is a fundamental aspect of the PHP language, and you should not rely on variables persisting between function calls unless you specifically design your code to do so.

