```c
void quickSort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivot = partition(arr, low, high);
        quickSort(arr, low, pivot-1);
        quickSort(arr, pivot+1, high);
    }
}

int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low-1;
    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        if (arr[j] > pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(arr[i+1], arr[high]);
    return i+1;
}
```

The quicksort algorithm works by selecting a pivot element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays, according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The sub-arrays are then recursively sorted. The partition function determines the pivot element and initializes a pointer to the last element in the left sub-array. The quicksort function then recursively sorts the left and right sub-arrays. The algorithm terminates when the sub-arrays are sorted or when the low and high indices have converged.

