Even if I not using created object, server returns 500 Internal Server Error and tell me:
Whoops!
We seem to have hit a snag. Please try again later...
What Im doing wrong?

That error is occurring because in order to use classes in PHP, you need to:

1. Define the class. In your case, you have the StubWelcomeMessageProvider class defined in Services/StubWelcomeMessageProvider.php.

2. Require/include the file that defines the class. You're missing this step.

So your controller should be:
```php
<?php

namespace App\Controllers;

require APPPATH . 'Services/StubWelcomeMessageProvider.php';

use Services\StubWelcomeMessageProvider;

class Home extends BaseController
{
    public function index()
    {
        $welcomeMessageProvider = new StubWelcomeMessageProvider();
        return 'hello world';
    }
}
```

The require APPPATH . 'Services/StubWelcomeMessageProvider.php'; line includes the file that defines the StubWelcomeMessageProvider class, making it available to use.

Without including the file that defines the class, PHP will give you that 500 error because it does not recognize the class.

So in summary, to use classes in PHP you need:

1. Define the class
2. Require/include the file that defines the class
3. Then you can instantiate and use the class

